  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Cool weather will linger into the weekend. Near \nto slightly above normal temperatures return next week.\n\nQuiet conditions continue to prevail across the region, as current \nobservations show calm winds and clear skies. Thus, with the aid of \nefficient radiational cooling, have seen temperatures fall into mid \n50s inland to lower 60s along the coast. These values will continue \nto fall in the coming hours, with locations further inland possibly \ndipping into the upper 40s prior to daybreak.\n\nTemperatures moderate fairly quickly this afternoon as upper level \nridging builds aloft, allowing highs to warm into the upper 70s to \nlower 80s, which is still a touch below normal for this time of \nyear. Otherwise, expect similar conditions to prevail this evening, \nas light winds and clear skies yield another chilly night. However, \nwinds out of the south/southwest should help cap lows in the mid \n50s. \n\nLooking ahead into next week, will see broad upper level ridging and \nsfc high pressure linger overhead, keeping conditions quiet across \nour area. Temperatures will continue to moderate during this time, \nwith highs in the upper 80s to low 90s likely again by mid-week. \nMoisture then increases Thursday into Friday along the backside of \nsubtropical high pressure centered well offshore over the Atlantic, \ncausing rain chances to return. Nonetheless, confidence remains too \nlow at this time to narrow in on any specifics. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: The coastal flood risk will linger into early next \nweek.\n\nBreezy north-northeasterly flow will continue across the region, as \na nor'easter churns offshore of the Mid-Atlantic coastline. This \ncombined with the recent full moon will result in minor (possibly \nmoderate) coastal flooding with each high tide cycle this weekend \n(esp. for the Charleston Harbor).\n\nCharleston Harbor: Coastal flooding concerns will continue today \n(9/26) with the potential to reach moderate flood stage for \nCharleston and Colleton County with the morning high tide (~8AM). \nThus, plan to issue a Coastal Flood Advisory with this forecast \npackage, which will be in effect from 6 AM until 10 AM. Thereafter, \nit's likely to see minor coastal flooding conditions through early \nnext week for the Charleston Harbor.\n\nFort Pulaski: Total water forecasts indicate this morning's and \nSunday morning's high tide could peak around 9.5 ft MLLW, which will \nbe close to yielding minor coastal flooding impacts across lower \nSouth Carolina and southeast Georgia. Given the borderline nature, \nwill continue to monitor trends in the coming hours and issue a \nCoastal Flood Advisory if warranted.",
